  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA robust low level mass response is underway to our west as a \nroughly 50-60 kt LLJ is gradually spreading north and east \nacross Iowa and southern Minnesota. This is occurring ahead of a\npotent shortwave carving through western Wyoming which will \neventually emerge across the Northern Plains later this \nafternoon. We'll get into the southern fringes of this LLJ later\ntoday which will result in an increase in southwesterly winds, \noccasionally gusting towards 25/sporadically nearing 30 mph \nacross northwest Illinois. A strong subsidence inversion \noverhead, combined with a steady increase in cirrus cloud cover,\nwill greatly limit mixing depths today which, thankfully, will \nalso help mute the RH fall and temper the magnitude of wind \ngusts today. Still, relatively dry conditions and gusty winds \nmay drive a slightly increased threat for field fires today. \nHigh temperatures should end up a few degrees warmer than \nTuesday, ending up in the upper 50s and lower 60s. \n\nA strong low-level wind field will pivot across the region \ntonight which will likely lead to sporadic 20-25 mph gusts \nparticularly across the urban corridor across northeast \nIllinois. Southwesterly breezes will continue on Thursday, with \nhigh temperatures getting another few degree boost into the mid \n60s area-wide. Did nudge wind gusts up a bit from the NBM- \ndelivered values given progged 950-900 mb flow remaining in the \n25-30 kt range. \n\nThe surface flow will begin to back slightly on Friday as the\nnext area of elongated surface troughing develops across the\nCentral Plains. Friday looks like another spectacular day \n(perhaps a bit breezy), with highs pushing well into the 60s \n(upper 60s in reach across our southwest). On Saturday, the GFS \nand some of its ensembles develop an initial area of enhanced \nlow and mid-level saturation ahead of an incoming cold front \ncommensurately squeeze out showers/drizzle across parts of the \narea during the afternoon. This outcome is not ubiquitous across\nthe guidance suite, however, with the multi-model consensus \npointing to a largely dry afternoon. \n\nPrecipitation chances will increase quickly Saturday evening\ninto Sunday morning as a neutral tilt shortwave slides overhead\nand the attendant surface cold front pushes through. The bulk \nof the precip with this system looks to come to an end through \nSunday afternoon. Depending on the orientation of the incoming \nhigh and amount of lingering lower-level moisture, some lake \neffect precipitation may develop into early next week although \nthis signal is not particularly strong in our area. Temperatures\nwill fall back to near and below normal during this time frame \nas well. \n\nCarlaw",
